# Run the server

`wyrd-server` is the registry: it stores cards, versions them, validates specs,
holds artifact bytes, and serves everything over HTTP, MCP, and the CLI. It needs
two things — a **Postgres** database and a **storage** backend — and is configured
entirely through environment variables.

This page has two tracks. Start with **Local dev** to get a server running on your
machine in a minute; read **Production** when you deploy.

## Local dev

In local mode the server manages everything for you: it starts an embedded
Postgres, provisions roles, runs migrations, and writes artifacts to the local
filesystem.

Leave all three `WYRD_DATABASE_*` variables unset to get embedded Postgres, and
point storage at a local directory:

```bash
export WYRD_STORAGE_BACKEND=local
export WYRD_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/wyrd-data   # must exist
export WYRD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

wyrd-server
```

That is the whole local setup. The server boots embedded Postgres, runs
migrations, builds its runtime pools, and starts serving.

<Aside title="First credential" data-variant="wyrd">

A fresh server has no principals. Mint the first credential before you make
authenticated calls — see [Authentication](/server/auth/) for issuing and
exchanging keys.

</Aside>

## Production

In production you bring your own Postgres and a cloud object store. Nothing else
about the binary changes.

### Postgres

Wyrd uses **one database and three login roles** — `wyrd_app` (RLS-bound runtime
queries), `wyrd_migrator` (boot-only DDL), and `wyrd_platform_admin` (optional
background sweepers and audited cross-tenant routes). The role names are a schema
contract baked into the migrations; they cannot be renamed per deploy.

You provide one canonical URL carrying the `wyrd_app` userinfo plus the per-role
passwords. The server synthesizes the migrator and platform-admin DSNs at boot by
swapping the userinfo.

| Variable | Required | Role |
|---|:---:|---|
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | yes | `wyrd_app` — full `postgres://user:pass@host:port/db` DSN |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD` | yes | `wyrd_migrator` — boot migrations only |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD` | no | `wyrd_platform_admin` — sweepers + audited routes |

Configuration is all-or-nothing: set the URL **and** the migrator password (and
optionally the platform-admin password) for external Postgres, or leave all three
unset for embedded mode. Partial configuration is rejected (`MixedDsnConfig`).

Infrastructure must provision the roles before the server starts. Run
`crates/wyrd/wyrd-sql/bootstrap/roles.sql` as a cluster superuser, substituting the
`REPLACE_WITH_*_PASSWORD` placeholders from your secret store. The migration path
validates that the roles exist with the expected `BYPASSRLS` posture and fails
early if bootstrap is incomplete. Pool tuning (`WYRD_DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS`,
`WYRD_DB_MIN_CONNECTIONS`, acquire/idle/lifetime timeouts) defaults sensibly; tune
per role with `_MIGRATOR` / `_PLATFORM_ADMIN` suffixes.

### Storage

Set `WYRD_STORAGE_BACKEND` to one of `local`, `s3`, `gcs`, or `azure`. The server
refuses to start without it. The client tier carries zero cloud SDKs — every
presign and upload completion runs server-side.

| Backend | Key variables |
|---|---|
| `s3` | `WYRD_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET`, optional `WYRD_STORAGE_S3_REGION`, `WYRD_STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT_URL`, `WYRD_STORAGE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE` |
| `gcs` | `WYRD_STORAGE_GCS_BUCKET` |
| `azure` | `WYRD_STORAGE_AZURE_ACCOUNT`, `WYRD_STORAGE_AZURE_CONTAINER` |
| `local` | `WYRD_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT`, `WYRD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` |

Credentials are **never** raw keys in config. Each cloud backend resolves them
through its provider's standard chain, so the same binary works under IAM roles,
GKE Workload Identity, or AKS Managed Identity with no code change. On Kubernetes,
prefer IRSA (S3), Workload Identity (GCS), or Managed Identity (Azure) over
long-lived secrets.

Set `WYRD_STORAGE_REQUIRE_ENCRYPTION=true` on cloud backends to reject uploads
whose objects are not encrypted at rest. Tune presign TTL and multipart part size
with `WYRD_STORAGE_PRESIGN_TTL_SECS` and `WYRD_STORAGE_PART_SIZE_BYTES` (raise the
part size for very large model artifacts).

### Boot order

On start, the server resolves env (or starts embedded Postgres), builds the
migrator pool, runs `wyrd-sql` then `vala-sql` migrations, closes the migrator
pool, builds the runtime pools, and begins serving with graceful shutdown. The
migrator DSN must connect directly to Postgres — do not route migrations through
transaction-mode PgBouncer.

